{#
  MKA policy and MACsec key chain for the pre-shared-key path.

  /native/mka/policy[name]/macsec-cipher-suite
  /native/key/chain[name]/macsec
  /native/key/chain[name]/key[id]/cryptographic-algorithm-choice/macsec
  /native/key/chain[name]/key[id]/key-string/key

  macsec-cipher-suite is not optional in practice. The default MKA policy gives you
  GCM-AES-256 nowhere: leave it out and the session comes up on GCM-AES-128.

  The key id is the CKN, verbatim. "01" here shows up as CKN 01 in "show mka
  sessions", which is how you tell a PSK session from an EAP-derived one at a glance:
  the EAP-TLS path produces a 32-hex-character CKN that changes on every re-auth.

  The key-string is the CAK. The YANG enforces what the CLI enforces: hex only, and
  exactly 64 characters for aes-256-cmac (32 for aes-128-cmac). Getting that wrong is
  rejected at the edit rather than silently producing a session that will not come up.

  THE KEY IS PUSHED ONCE, ON THE RUN THAT CREATES THE CHAIN.

  macsec_psk_push_key_string comes from tasks/main.yml, which probes the box for the
  key chain first. Re-pushing a plaintext key-string onto a chain that already exists
  does not merely churn, it FAILS: the box has stored the CAK encrypted, so the
  running config carries "key-string 7 <blob>", and the NETCONF-to-CLI layer renders
  our plaintext against that "7". "key-string 7 <plaintext hex>" is rejected with
  "Rejecting: Key must consist of hex digits". Pushing the chain and the cipher suite
  again is fine; it is only this leaf that cannot be replayed.

  The cost of the skip is documented on the probe task and in DESIGN.md: a rotated
  CAK needs a teardown with -e macsec_psk_remove_globals=true first. It defaults to
  true so the teardown path, which renders this same template with
  nc:operation="remove", is unaffected.

  LIFETIME: macsec.md also sets "lifetime local 00:00:00 Jan 1 2020 infinite" on the
  key. It is NOT true that this cannot be modelled, though two of the three obvious
  paths are dead ends. accept-lifetime and send-lifetime both carry
  when 'not(../../macsec)' and are rejected here with unknown-element, and the older
  lifetime / lifetime-local choice is status obsolete and also rejected. The live path
  is a third container, macsec-lifetime/lifetime, gated the other way round with
  when '../../../macsec':

    <macsec-lifetime><lifetime><lifetime-group-v1>
      <local/><start-hh-mm-ss>00:00:00</start-hh-mm-ss>
      <start-month>Jan</start-month><start-day>1</start-day>
      <start-year>2020</start-year><infinite/>
    </lifetime-group-v1></lifetime></macsec-lifetime>

  Verified on 26.2.1: that payload is accepted and renders exactly
  "lifetime local 00:00:00 Jan 1 2020 infinite".

  It is deliberately left out below, because a key with no lifetime is always valid and
  the shorter payload is one less thing to get wrong. Add the container above if you
  want byte-for-byte parity with the doc.
-#}
{% set op = ' nc:operation="remove"' if state == 'absent' else '' %}
<config xmlns="urn:ietf:params:xml:ns:netconf:base:1.0"
        xmlns:nc="urn:ietf:params:xml:ns:netconf:base:1.0">
  <native xmlns="http://cisco.com/ns/yang/Cisco-IOS-XE-native">
    <mka>
      <policy xmlns="http://cisco.com/ns/yang/Cisco-IOS-XE-mka"{{ op }}>
        <name>{{ macsec_names.psk_mka_policy }}</name>
{% if state == 'present' %}
        <macsec-cipher-suite>{{ macsec_cipher_suite }}</macsec-cipher-suite>
{% endif %}
      </policy>
    </mka>
    <key>
      <chain xmlns="http://cisco.com/ns/yang/Cisco-IOS-XE-crypto"{{ op }}>
        <name>{{ macsec_names.psk_key_chain }}</name>
{% if state == 'present' %}
        <macsec/>
{% if macsec_psk_push_key_string | default(true) | bool %}
        <key>
          <id>{{ macsec_key_id }}</id>
          <cryptographic-algorithm-choice>
            <macsec>{{ macsec_key_algorithm }}</macsec>
          </cryptographic-algorithm-choice>
          <key-string>
            <key>{{ lab_macsec_cak }}</key>
          </key-string>
        </key>
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
      </chain>
    </key>
  </native>
</config>
